In this instance the demos ranged from small to very wide grids and used disparate palettes and structures. Observed effects included narrow line fills around isolated markers, multi-directional fills/rays, and replacement of marker cells with 4; one demo also altered a uniform-color region into a rectangular extension. The test had sparse color-3 cells plus color-2 triplets on opposite borders. I inferred a vertical extension from one marker and converted all markers to 4, but that candidate was rejected. The next attempt assumed horizontal fills from the edge runs and was also rejected. These outputs were not validated by exact replay against all demos, and the examples were too heterogeneous to justify transferring that simple rule confidently.

Lesson: after an incorrect submission, do not select one visually appealing delta from the latest demo and apply it to the test. Compare the entire demo set, check dimensions/palette/task-class coherence, and explicitly derive a rule that predicts all changed and unchanged cells. For request decisions, state competing hypotheses and the exact observation needed to distinguish them; additional examples without a specific discriminating purpose are costly but may still fail to make a drifting/non-identifiable mapping coherent. Do not present a speculative grid as evidence-based.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u042/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md"}

